{% comment %}
=====================================================================
 {% chart %} — the same report, drawn twice, written once
 ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 Compare this with examples/sample_report_template.liquid, which draws
 one chart. That one needs: a <canvas> with a hand-picked width and
 height, a <script> that loads Chart.js from a CDN, a Chart.js config,
 a data array assembled from Liquid strings, a window.status handshake
 so the PDF engine knows when to snapshot, and a responsive:false that
 exists because of one PDF engine's 2011 WebKit.

 Six things. Five of them are facts about the RENDERER, and none of
 them is something the person writing a report should have to know.

 This template has none of them. Every chart below is one line, and
 the plugin decides the rest:

   * in a browser  -> a <canvas> plus a <script type="application/json">
                      data block, and the bundled Chart.js 4 draws it
   * in a PDF      -> an inline <svg> computed on the server: vector,
                      selectable, no JavaScript at all, and every bar
                      is a real link

 Both are laid out by ONE calculation, so the axis, the ticks, the
 colours and the plot rectangle are the same in the two documents.
 That is measured rather than asserted — see
 spec/charts/shared_layout_falsifier_spec.rb, which renders this exact
 kind of chart in a real browser and compares the plot area with the
 server's to within 2%.

 WHICH RENDERER. {% chart %} needs this plugin's own report renderer.
 Pasted into a Reporter report template it records the chart and emits
 a placeholder that Reporter's renderer does not fill, so use it with
 the plugin's own preview and reports.

 Source query: an "issues" report.
=====================================================================
{% endcomment %}

{% comment %} 1) Three aggregations, all in SQL, no per-issue loop anywhere. {% endcomment %}
{% sql_aggregate from: issues, group_by: status, drill: true, assign_to: by_status %}
{% sql_aggregate from: issues, group_by: assignee, sort: count, limit: 12, drill: true, assign_to: by_assignee %}
{% sql_aggregate from: issues, period: month, periods: 6, assign_to: per_month %}

<div class="rrd-report-head">
  <h1>Issue report</h1>
  <p>{{ by_status.total }} issues in scope</p>
</div>

{% comment %}
 2) A vertical bar. `drill: true` on the aggregation above put a URL on every
    bucket, and the chart carries them through — as <a xlink:href> in the SVG
    and as a click handler in the browser. The author writes neither.
{% endcomment %}
{% chart id: status, from: by_status, title: "Issues by status", y_title: "Issues" %}

{% comment %}
 3) A horizontal bar, because assignee names are long. `orientation` is the only
    thing that changes; the layout reserves the label width on the correct side.
{% endcomment %}
{% chart id: assignee, from: by_assignee, orientation: horizontal, width: 640, height: 420,
   title: "Open issues by assignee" %}

{% comment %}
 4) A line over the period series. `y:` names which of the parallel arrays to
    draw — the aggregator also returns open_now, and putting it on this axis
    would be a chart nobody asked for.
{% endcomment %}
{% chart id: trend, from: per_month, type: line, y: "created,closed",
   title: "Created and closed", x_title: "Month" %}

{% comment %}
 5) The same status breakdown as a pie, to show that the type is the only
    difference. A pie legend keys on the CATEGORIES, so the slices are labelled
    rather than being four colours a reader has to guess at.
{% endcomment %}
{% chart id: mix, from: by_status, type: pie, width: 420, height: 320,
   title: "Share of issues by status" %}

<p class="rrd-report-note">
  Every chart above is described in text as well as drawn: the SVG carries a
  &lt;title&gt; and a &lt;desc&gt; with the numbers in them, and the canvas carries the
  same sentence as an aria-label. A reader using a screen reader, and a reader
  looking at a greyscale print, both get the findings.
</p>
